course: dictionary
lesson: 13913
title: Lawful and the law
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Lawful is an adjective. It describes something that is allowed by the law, or legal. «Selling lemonade on the pavement is lawful in our town, because the council says yes.» So lawful means legal.
explain: Lawful can also mean obeying the law or following the rules. «The lawful choice was to wait at the red light.» Here the word tells you that waiting was the right thing to do by the rules.
explain: Look for the opposite to help you remember. Unlawful means breaking the law. «Taking a toy from a shop without paying is unlawful.» Lawful and unlawful are a pair, like happy and unhappy.
fact: Lawful is an adjective: it describes a noun, such as a lawful action.
fact: «Lawful» means allowed by the law, or legal.
fact: «Lawful» can also mean obeying the law or following the rules.
fact: The opposite of lawful is unlawful, which means breaking the law.
fact: Phrases you may meet are lawful owner, lawful right and lawful behaviour.
exercise: «The lawful owner of the bike asked for it back.» What does lawful owner mean? | *The real owner, recognised by the law | A bike that is very old | An owner who is very tall | An owner who has lost the bike
exercise: Which word is the opposite of lawful? | cheerful | helpful | *unlawful | useful
exercise: Which action is lawful? | Driving through a red light | Taking a toy from a shop without paying | *Crossing the road when the light is green
test: «Selling lemonade is lawful in our town.» What does lawful mean here? | Very cold | Very quiet | Lost | *Allowed by the law
test: Which word means breaking the law? | Lovely | Lawn | *Unlawful | Lawful
exam: Lilly says, «The police must act in a lawful way.» What does lawful way mean? | Running very fast | Playing a game | Shouting loudly | *Following the law and the rules
